    {
      "id": "30000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000009",
      "name": "Sticky Note",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "typeVersion": 1,
      "position": [
        -200,
        -60
      ],
      "parameters": {
        "width": 2560,
        "height": 200,
        "content": "## Notify team\nComposes a plain-text alert (so patient-written text can't inject markup) and emails config.team_email. A per-phone cooldown keeps one phone from using up the Gmail daily quota that real emergencies rely on: unless the reason is tool_failure (a system failure the team must always hear about), Check alert cooldown asks whether any of this phone's inbound messages within config.limits.alert_cooldown_minutes is marked team_alerted, and if so returns { ok: true, suppressed: true } without sending. An emergency is held back only by an earlier emergency, so an earlier alert of another kind, such as a question about an unlisted treatment, never keeps a dental emergency from the team; every other reason is held back by any earlier alert. Suppressed means an earlier alert reached the team inside the window, not this one, so a caller must not tell the patient this request was forwarded. Check alert cooldown setting first refuses an alert_cooldown_minutes that isn't an integer of 1 or more, into a Stop and Error the error workflow reports, instead of silently turning the cooldown off; tool_failure alerts skip it and still send. A cooldown check that itself fails sends anyway. After a send, Mark team alerted marks the caller's message_id with team_alerted and the alert's reason as alerted_reason, which the cooldown reads back (callers without a message_id mark nothing), and a failed mark still returns { ok: true }. Returns { ok: true }, or { ok: false, reason: 'email_failed' } when Gmail's error output fires."
      }
    }
